          %h1#intuitionistic_mathematics Intuitionistic mathematics
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Terminology"} Terminology
              %li
                %a{:href => "#mathematical_principles"} Mathematical principles
              %li
                %a{:href => "#feel"} Feel
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            %em Intuitionistic mathematics
            (often abbreviated INT) is the earliest full-blown variety of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.E.J.+Brouwer"} L.E.J. Brouwer
            through his philosophy of
            = succeed "." do
              %em intuitionism
          .standout
            %p
              Beware that this terminology is not consistent across mathematics. Not infrequently the word “intuitionistic” is used to refer simply to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
              in general, or to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive logic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} impredicative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
              done in constructive logic. This page is about
              = succeed "," do
                %em Brouwer’s intuitionism
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+constructive+mathematics"} neutral constructive mathematics
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contradiction"} contradict
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Terminology"} Terminology
              below.
          %p
            The first main philosophical idea is that mathematical truth (a true statement) can only be attained in one’s mind, by carefully arranging one’s concepts and constructions in such a way that there remains absolutely no doubt that every aspect of the statement is verified, unambiguously, without reliance on any ‘outside’ assumption, for instance about the platonic nature of reality. (The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
            is such an assumption, and Brouwer gave mathematical counterexamples to its validity, eventually leading to the foundational crisis in mathematics (Grundlagenstreit) around 1930, and Brouwer’s conflict with Hilbert).
          %p The second main philosophical idea is that the mind works over time. One does not have everything ready and done from the start. Infinity is a (perceived) idealized property of time, but one cannot have completed any infinite process or construction on any given day. Infinity is potential, not actual.
          %p
            The main mathematical idea then becomes that we can only build mathematical structures and truths starting from the natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0, 1, ...
            (where the dots indicate potential, not actual infinity). Natural numbers are definite and precise, but most real numbers, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %p
            Nonetheless, a substantial part of mathematics can be built up (‘constructed’) in this way. What mainly differentiates intuitionistic mathematics from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            are two added axioms.
          %p Brouwer ‘deduced’ two axiomatic insights, notably ‘continuous choice’ and a transfinite-induction based knowledge principle called `the Bar Theorem'. 'Continuous choice' conflicts with classical mathematics, the Bar Theorem is classically true (it boils down to stating that any open cover of Baire space is inductive).
          %p
            Kleene &amp; Vesley (in
            = succeed "," do
              %em Foundations of Intuitionistic Mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability"} realizable
          %h2#Terminology Terminology
          %p
            Terminological ambiguity is often present in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            and its varieties. Intuitionistic mathematics (INT) includes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            that contradict
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
          %p There are a variety of ways to use the term ‘intuitionistic’. We list them here, roughly from the most specific to the most general, and contrast (where appropriate) with the term ‘constructive’:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Intuitionism
                is an early-20th-century
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy+of+mathematics"} philosophy of mathematics
                developed by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer"} Brouwer
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
                is a free creation of a mind, and valid results are about what that mind creates (rather than about an external reality, as in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/platonism"} platonism
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formalism"} formalism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
                (which he saw as derived from mathematics, rather than conversely as in
                = succeed ")." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    logicism
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/logicism"} ?
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructivism"} constructivism
            %li
              %p
                %strong Intuitionistic mathematics
                is the mathematics along the lines of the mathematics that Brouwer came up with. However, it's not necessary to accept Brouwer's philosophy to practise intuitionistic mathematics; conversely, one may accept Brouwer's philosophical starting place but not his conclusions about the resulting mathematics. Intuitionistic mathematics is one particular variety of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
              %p
                One example of intuitionistic mathematics (which nicely shows that intuitionism is not a matter of “belief” but of subject) is type II
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computable+mathematics"} computable mathematics
                (see for instance
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Bauer05"} Bauer 05, section 4.3.1
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    Intuitionistic set theory
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/intuitionistic+set+theory"} ?
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/IZF"} intuitionistic Zermelo-Frankel set theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
                fails but
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
                are included (making it
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+mathematics"} impredicative
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CZF"} constructive Zermelo-Frankel set theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
                but not power sets (making it
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+predicative+mathematics"} weakly predicative
                %em both
                of these set theories stronger than he would accept.
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} Intuitionistic type theory
                is generally proffered as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
                that is (in most of its forms) both
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructivism"} constructive
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicativism"} predicative
          %p
            There is variant of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NuPrl"} NuPrl
            type theory with choice sequences:
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.nuprl.org/html/Nuprl2Coq/continuity.pdf"} PDF
          %ul
            %li
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Intuitionistic+logic"} Intuitionistic logic
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
              that intuitionistic mathematics, set theory, and type theory use, which lacks the principle of
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
              also use intuitionistic logic, which is therefore also known as
              = succeed "." do
                %em constructive logic
          %h2#mathematical_principles Mathematical principles
          %p
            Brouwer did not believe in a rigourous formalization of mathematics, for various reasons (amongst which the mathematical incompleteness of formal systems, as later proved by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%B6del%27s+incompleteness+theorem"} Gödel's incompleteness theorem
          %p
            Brouwer’s student Arend Heyting (who later succeeded Brouwer as professor in Amsterdam) formalized what is now known as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer-Heyting-Kolmogorov+interpretation"} Brouwer-Heyting-Kolmogorov interpretation
            (BHK) of intuitionistic logic, BHK can be seen as a precursor to realizability.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In hindsight, we may say that intuitionistic mathematics is done in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
                identified as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %li
              %p
                We have the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+choice"} countable choice
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %li
              %p
                We have the classically false principles of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuity+principle"} continuity
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    continuous choice
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/continuous+choice"} ?
            %li
              %p
                We have the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fan+theorem"} fan theorem
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+theorem"} bar theorem
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russian+constructivism"} Russian constructivism
            %li
              %p
                There's also some stuff about
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+sequences"} choice sequences
                that is highly philosophical, involving mathematical principles such as the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke%27s+schema"} Kripke's schema
          %p
            Although it's not enough to derive all of the above, much of the essence of intuitionistic mathematics, or at least intuitionistic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+continuous+map"} uniformly continuous
          %p
            Intuitionistic mathematics is often regarded as a specialization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop%27s+constructive+mathematics"} Bishop's constructive mathematics
            obtained by adding the above principles, but this is somewhat questionable if it refers to what Bishop actually did (and in particular the fact that he worked with
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop+sets"} Bishop sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop%27s+constructive+mathematics"} Bishop's constructive mathematics
            for discussion.
          %h2#feel Feel
          %p
            In intuitionistic mathematics, already
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            behaves a lot like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franka+Waaldijk"} Franka Waaldijk
            (
            %a{:href => "http://www.fwaaldijk.nl/mathematics.html"}> web
            ). She uses the
            %span.newWikiWord
              Kleene-Vesley
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Kleene-Vesley"} ?
            system. Fourman’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+truth"} continuous truth
            makes this remark precise using
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
          %p
            Although intuitionistic mathematics does not accept all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            (much less
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductive
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinduction"} coinductive
            structures; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/12/the_status_of_coalgebra.html#c020731"} a Café comment
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+category+of+spaces"} nice category of spaces
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BHK+interpretation"} BHK interpretation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability"} realizability
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+sequence"} choice sequence
          %h2#References References
          %p
            For more see also the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %p The roots of Brouwer’s intuitionism are apparently in his PhD thesis
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.E.J.+Brouwer"}> L.E.J. Brouwer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Over de Grondslagen der Wiskunde, PhD thesis
          %p A reference written by Brouwer and still in print in English is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.E.J.+Brouwer"}> L.E.J. Brouwer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Brouwer’s Cambridge Lectures on Intuitionism
          %p Early exposition and endorsement:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"}> Hermann Weyl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Über die neue Grundlagenkrise der Mathematik
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/id/PPN266833020_0010?tify={%22pages%22:[45],%22panX%22:0.525,%22panY%22:0.381,%22view%22:%22info%22,%22zoom%22:0.659}"} gdz
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://resolver.sub.uni-goettingen.de/purl?PPN266833020_0010"} purl:PPN266833020_0010
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"}> Hermann Weyl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Die heutige Erkenntnislage in der Mathematik
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://books.google.de/books/about/Die_heutige_Erkenntnislage_in_der_Mathem.html?id=1KU_AQAAIAAJ&redir_esc=y"} GoogleBooks
          %p
            Original articles on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"} intuitionism
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"}> intuitionistic logic
            ):
          %ul
            %li#Heyting1930
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                %em Die formalen Regeln der intuitionistischen Logik. I, II, III.
                Sitzungsberichte der Preußischen Akademie der Wissenschaften, Physikalisch-Mathematische Klasse (1930) 42-56, 57-71, 158-169
              %p abridged reprint in:
              %p
                Karel Berka, Lothar Kreiser (eds.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Logik-Texte
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9783112645826"} doi:10.1515/9783112645826
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Die intuitionistische Grundlegung der Mathematik
                %strong 2
                (1931) 106-115 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/20011630"}> jsotr:20011630
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.psiquadrat.de/downloads/heyting1931.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrey+Kolmogorov"}> Andrey Kolmogorov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Zur Deutung der intuitionistischen Logik
                %strong 35
                (1932) 58-65 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01186549"}> doi:10.1007/BF01186549
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Freudenthal"}> Hans Freudenthal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Zur intuitionistischen Deutung logischer Formeln
                %strong 4
                (1937) 112-116 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=CM_1937__4__112_0"}> numdam:CM_1937__4__112_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bemerkungen zu dem Aufsatz von Herrn Freudenthal “Zur intuitionistischen Deutung logischer Formeln”
                %strong 4
                (1937) 117-118 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=CM_1937__4__117_0"}> doi:CM_1937__4__117_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L.+E.+J.+Brouwer"}> L. E. J. Brouwer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Points and Spaces
                %strong 6
                (1954) 1-17 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1954-001-9"}> doi:10.4153/CJM-1954-001-9
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Heyting56
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"}> Arend Heyting
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Intuitionism: An introduction
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => ""} ISBN:978-0720422399
            %li#Kreisel65
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Kreisel"}> Georg Kreisel
                , Section 2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on Modern Mathematics III
          %p
            Formalization in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability"} realizability
            (the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleene-Vesley+topos"} Kleene-Vesley topos
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Kleene"}> Stephen Kleene
              , R. E. Vesley,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The foundations of intuitionistic mathematics
          %p General review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/intuitionism/"} Intuitionism in the Philosophy of Mathematics
            %li
              %p
                Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://iep.utm.edu/intuitionism-math/"} Intuitionism in Mathematics
          %p
            See also texts on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anne+Sjerp+Troelstra"}> Anne Sjerp Troelstra
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirk+van+Dalen"} Dirk van Dalen
              = succeed "," do
                %em Constructivism in Mathematics – An introduction
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 121
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.elsevier.com/books/constructivism-in-mathematics-vol-1/troelstra/978-0-444-70266-1"} ISBN:9780444702661
          %p Reviews with further developments:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franka+Waaldijk"}> Franka Waaldijk
                ,
                %em On the foundations of constructive mathematics – especially in relation to the theory of continuous functions
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.fwaaldijk.nl/mathematics.html#onthefoundations"}> web
                )
              %p
                (with a focus on
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+analysis"} constructive analysis
          %p
            Discussion of an approach to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+topology"} general topology
            in intuitionistic mathematics:
          %ul
            %li#Waaldijk96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franka+Waaldijk"}> Franka Waaldijk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em modern intuitionistic topology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.fwaaldijk.nl/modern%20intuitionistic%20topology.pdf"} pdf
        .revisedby
